Fix VAP reconfiguration: stop the AP before restarting it, and never emit an
out-of-range MLO link id.

THE SYMPTOM this fixes, end to end: a BananaPi RDK-B controller could never
apply an EasyMesh-provided SSID to its own radios. The whole 1905/WSC exchange
worked, the configuration arrived correctly, OneWifi decoded and dispatched it
correctly, and the radios kept beaconing the image default SSID forever -- so
the controller and its extender never shared a fronthaul ESS and client roaming
between them could not be tested at all.

Two defects in this file, in one code path. They must be fixed together: either
one alone changes nothing, which was confirmed by testing each in isolation.

DEFECT 1 -- restart_interface() starts an AP that is already running.

    interface->beacon_set = 0;              /* forces the START_AP path */
    if (start_bss(interface) < 0) { ... }   /* but the AP is still running */

beacon_set is what wifi_drv_set_ap() uses to choose between reconfiguring a
live AP and starting one:

    beacon_set = params->reenable ? 0 : interface->beacon_set;
    if (beacon_set) { cmd = NL80211_CMD_SET_BEACON; }   /* else START_AP */
    if (beacon_set == 0) { nl80211_fill_chandef(msg, radio, interface); }

restart_interface() zeroes it unconditionally and never issues
NL80211_CMD_STOP_AP, so the HAL asks mac80211 to START an AP that is already
started, and gets:

    wifi_drv_set_ap:17523: Failed to set beacon parameter for interface: wifi1
        error: -114(Operation already in progress)
    start_bss:3423: vap:private_ssid_5g:1 create is failed:-1
    restart_interface:6307: interface:wifi1 failed to start BSS

-114 is EALREADY. params->reenable is not involved -- that is set only on the
DFS path -- interface->beacon_set is genuinely 0 while the AP runs.

The stop here is deliberately UNCONDITIONAL rather than guarded on
interface->bss_started: that flag was observed false while the AP was up and
beaconing, so guarding on it skips the stop in exactly the case that needs it.
Stopping an interface with no AP running returns a harmless error (-2 ENOENT),
which is logged at debug level and ignored.

DEFECT 2 -- the required STOP_AP was itself rejected, with -34 ERANGE.

Dumping every attribute emitted immediately before the send separates the
harmless case from the real one, on the same interface:

    link_id=-1  (NA) emitted=no   attr IFINDEX=16   -> -2 ENOENT   (no AP yet)
    link_id=255 (NA=-1) emitted=YES attr IFINDEX=16 -> -34 ERANGE

The cause is two different "unset" sentinels being conflated:

    #define NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_NA (-1)

    wifi_hal_get_mld_link_id():
        if (!wifi_hal_is_mld_enabled(interface)) return NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_NA;
        if (vap_mode == ap) return ...mld_info.common_info.mld_link_id;  /* 255 */

    nl80211_enable_ap():
        if (link_id != NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_NA &&
            nla_put_u8(msg, NL80211_ATTR_MLO_LINK_ID, link_id) < 0)

mld_link_id is an unsigned char left at 255 when no MLO link has been
established -- the same 255 that appears as "MLD_Link_ID": 255 in the OneWifi
VAP JSON. MLD is enabled in configuration here but never actually negotiated,
so the accessor returns 255, the caller's not-applicable test only knows about
-1, and 255 is emitted as a real link id. nl80211 range-checks
NL80211_ATTR_MLO_LINK_ID (IEEE 802.11be allows at most 15 links, so 0..14) and
rejects the ENTIRE message with -ERANGE.

Fixed in the accessor rather than at the emit site, so every caller is covered
at once. A defensive range check at each NL80211_ATTR_MLO_LINK_ID put would
also be worth having, for paths that read mld_link_id directly.

This is the same underlying condition as patch 0017 -- MLD configured but not
established -- surfacing in a different code path.

VERIFIED on a controller and its extender:

    before   wifi0/wifi1: BPI-RDKB-MLO-AP-, "create is failed:-1", -114 EALREADY
    after    wifi0/wifi1: private_ssid, zero create failures, zero -114, zero -34

and the client now sees a genuine shared ESS across both nodes and both bands:

    BSS 02:00:00:83:91:65 -- associated   SSID: private_ssid   extender 5GHz
    BSS 02:00:00:bf:ee:16                 SSID: private_ssid   extender 2.4GHz
    BSS 02:00:00:85:96:09                 SSID: private_ssid   controller 5GHz
    BSS 02:00:00:cb:bd:25                 SSID: private_ssid   controller 2.4GHz

NOT gated behind HWSIM_RADIO. Whether real hardware avoids either defect --
a vendor driver tolerating START_AP on a running AP, or MLD genuinely
establishing so mld_link_id holds a valid 0..14 -- is UNTESTED. That would
explain why both survive upstream, and it is the open question for anyone
considering this for a hardware build.

---
--- a/wifi_hal_nl80211_utils.c
+++ b/wifi_hal_nl80211_utils.c
@@ -5872,6 +5873,15 @@ int wifi_hal_set_mld_enabled(wifi_interface_info_t *interface, bool enabled)
 #define NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_NA (-1)
 #endif
 
+// IEEE 802.11be allows at most 15 links, so nl80211 accepts link IDs 0..14 and
+// range-checks NL80211_ATTR_MLO_LINK_ID against that. Anything above it is not a
+// link id but the data model's "unset" marker -- mld_link_id is an unsigned char
+// left at 255 when no MLO link has been established, which is what appears as
+// "MLD_Link_ID": 255 in the OneWifi VAP JSON.
+#ifndef NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_MAX
+#define NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_MAX (14)
+#endif
+
 int wifi_hal_get_mld_link_id(wifi_interface_info_t *interface)
 {
     if (interface == NULL) {
@@ -5884,7 +5894,19 @@ int wifi_hal_get_mld_link_id(wifi_interface_info_t *interface)
     }
 
     if (interface->vap_info.vap_mode == wifi_vap_mode_ap) {
-        return interface->vap_info.u.bss_info.mld_info.common_info.mld_link_id;
+        unsigned char link_id = interface->vap_info.u.bss_info.mld_info.common_info.mld_link_id;
+
+        // Normalise the data model's unset marker to this file's own, so callers
+        // that only test against NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_NA behave correctly. Without
+        // this they see 255, conclude a link id is applicable, and emit
+        // NL80211_ATTR_MLO_LINK_ID = 255 -- which the kernel range-checks and
+        // rejects, failing the ENTIRE netlink message with -ERANGE. That is not a
+        // theoretical concern: it is why NL80211_CMD_STOP_AP could not stop a
+        // running AP here, which in turn made every VAP reconfiguration fail.
+        if (link_id > NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_MAX) {
+            return NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_NA;
+        }
+        return link_id;
     }
 
     return NL80211_DRV_LINK_ID_NA;
@@ -6301,6 +6323,23 @@ int restart_interface(wifi_interface_info_t *interface)
     if (interface->vap_info.u.bss_info.enabled && radio->configured && radio->oper_param.enable &&
         interface->vap_initialized == true) {
         wifi_hal_info_print("%s:%d: interface:%s enable ap\n", __func__, __LINE__, interface_name);
+
+        // Stop the AP before restarting it. Zeroing beacon_set below makes
+        // wifi_drv_set_ap() use NL80211_CMD_START_AP rather than SET_BEACON, and
+        // mac80211 rejects START_AP on an interface whose AP is already running
+        // with -114 EALREADY -- so without this the restart fails and the VAP
+        // keeps its previous SSID and key.
+        //
+        // Deliberately unconditional: interface->bss_started was observed false
+        // while the AP was up and beaconing, so guarding on it skips the stop in
+        // exactly the case that needs it. Stopping an interface with no AP
+        // running returns a harmless error which is logged and ignored.
+        if (nl80211_enable_ap(interface, false) < 0) {
+            wifi_hal_dbg_print("%s:%d: interface:%s stop AP before restart returned an "
+                "error (expected when no AP was running)\n", __func__, __LINE__, interface_name);
+        }
+        interface->bss_started = false;
+
         interface->beacon_set = 0;
         if (start_bss(interface) < 0) {
             wifi_hal_error_print("%s:%d: interface:%s failed to start BSS\n", __func__, __LINE__,
